      "title": "Trinity Altarpiece",
      "year": 1478,
      "imageUrl": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/93/Hugo_van_der_Goes_-_The_Trinity_Altarpiece.jpg",
      "imageWidth": 2000,
      "imageHeight": 1016,
      "story": "The Trinity Altarpiece, also known as the Trinity Altar Panels, is a set of four paintings in oil on wood thought to have been commissioned in the late fifteenth century for the Trinity College Kirk in Edinburgh, Scotland. The panels are now part of the British Royal Collection and are loaned to the Scottish National Gallery.",
      "facts": [
        "He may have commissioned the altarpiece to strengthen ties of the Trinity Collegiate Church with Margaret of Denmark, and the imagery used may express her interests and personal iconography.",
        "The painting was taken to England soon after the Union of the Crowns and in 1617 was recorded in the collection of Anne of Denmark, wife of James VI and I, at Oatlands, the panels unjointed and as yet \"unhanged\"."
